# Postgres synonym dictionary (TEMPLATE = synonym): "source target" — a query for `source` is
# rewritten to lexeme `target` before matching, and — this is the part that is easy to miss —
# the SAME rewrite runs at INDEX time too, because indexing and querying both go through the
# `book_synonym_search` text-search configuration. A document containing the literal word
# `target` is not a dictionary key, so it stays indexed as `target`; a document containing
# literal `source` is rewritten to `target` at index time. Both ends converge on `target`,
# which is what makes `source` find a document that only says `target`.
#
# ONE DIRECTION PER PAIR ONLY. Listing both "source target" AND "target source" looks
# symmetric but is self-cancelling — and not for the reason it first appears. The same map runs
# on both sides, so each word is swapped for the OTHER one: the document says `source` and
# indexes as `target`, the query says `target` and is rewritten to `source`. Both sides move,
# and they still never meet. The dictionary becomes a no-op that costs you the feature.
# Verified against real Postgres 18 in GeneratedColumnAndSynonymDirectionIT, which builds
# scratch dictionaries from the pair `detective sleuth`: with both directions present neither
# word finds the other (both `f`); with one direction kept, both do (`t`).
#
# EVERY TARGET BELOW OCCURS IN THE SEEDED CORPUS AND EVERY SOURCE DOES NOT. That is the whole
# demonstration: you type a word no book contains and still get books back. A pair whose target
# is absent silently does nothing, which looks identical to the feature being broken —
# SynonymDictionaryCorpusIT is the guard that keeps this file and the corpus in step.
#
# AFTER EDITING THIS FILE: each session reads it once, the first time it uses the dictionary —
# sessions that already used it keep the old mapping until ALTER TEXT SEARCH DICTIONARY forces a
# reload, and idx_book_synonym_vector (001-schema.xml, changeset 009) still holds lexemes built
# from the old mapping until it is REINDEXed. Until both happen the index and the query disagree,
# silently. For this demo, `mise run infra:down && mise run demo` does both at once.
avenue street
brook river
casement window
chamber room
apothecary doctor
vessel ship
